Ran `crimes context src/Card.tsx` against the fixture. Beyond the three findings it repeats from the scan, the context adds the facts that decide *how* to sequence the refactor:

- **no tests found** — no siblings, no `__tests__/`, nothing importing it; `test_gap` is top-quartile (1.0) on all three findings.
- **0 direct importers, churn low** (2 commits/90d, last touched 3 months ago) — nothing downstream breaks, so this is the cheapest moment to change the component's shape.
- **`tailwind.config.ts` exists** with a real token scale (`brand`, `surface`, `ink`) — so the design system isn't missing, the file is bypassing it. That's what makes finding 2 a *token escape* rather than "no tokens yet".

Two things I found reading the file that the detectors don't say outright:

1. `width: 800` with `maxWidth: 1024` — the `maxWidth` never binds (800 < 1024). The fixed width wins, so the "responsive" intent is already dead code.
2. Inner widths `720 + 240 = 960` inside an 800px parent — this overflows *at desktop*, not just on mobile. `responsive_fragility` is understating it.

## The refactor

### 1. `accessible_interaction_risk` (medium, conf 0.85) — line 10/22

The naive fix (`role="button" tabIndex={0}` on the existing div) is wrong here, because this div wraps arbitrary `children`. If a caller passes a link or an input, you get an interactive control nested inside an interactive control — invalid, and screen readers flatten it. `role="button"` also doesn't get you Enter/Space handling; you'd have to write the `onKeyDown` yourself.

So: keep the container presentational and put a **real `<button>`** inside as the affordance. That also fixes the second half of the smell — `onClick={() => console.log("clicked")}` is a placeholder; the click target should be a prop.

```tsx
type CardProps = {
  children: React.ReactNode;
  onSelect?: () => void;
  selectLabel?: string;
};

export function Card({ children, onSelect, selectLabel = "Open card" }: CardProps) {
  return (
    <section className="@container w-full max-w-4xl rounded-lg border border-border bg-surface p-6 text-ink shadow-card">
      <div className="grid grid-cols-1 gap-6 @2xl:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_15rem]">
        <div className="min-w-0 text-base @2xl:text-lg text-ink-muted">{children}</div>
        <aside className="min-w-0 text-sm text-ink-subtle">sidebar</aside>
      </div>
      {onSelect && (
        <button
          type="button"
          onClick={onSelect}
          className="mt-4 rounded focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-focus"
        >
          {selectLabel}
        </button>
      )}
    </section>
  );
}
```

If whole-card click is a hard product requirement, the fallback is `role="button"` + `tabIndex={0}` + `aria-label` + an `onKeyDown` that fires on `Enter`/`Space` (and `preventDefault` on Space to stop page scroll) — **and** a documented constraint that `children` must be non-interactive. That's strictly worse; take it only if the design demands it.

### 2. `design_token_escape` (low, conf 0.75) — 9 raw values, line 10/24

Moving to Tailwind classes removes the raw values from JSX entirely, which is the durable fix — the point of this finding is that an agent extending the file copies `#333333` forward. But the swap isn't 1:1: the existing token set covers 3 of the 9 values. Extend the config first:

```ts
export default {
  theme: {
    colors: {
      brand: "#0050ff",
      surface: "#f7f7fa",
      ink: "#111111",
      "ink-muted": "#333333",    // was line 24
      "ink-subtle": "#666666",   // was line 27
      border: "#cccccc",         // was borderColor
      focus: "#1a73e8",          // was outlineColor
    },
    boxShadow: { card: "0 2px 4px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.2)" }, // was the rgb() literal
  },
};
```

One value resists tokenising: **`background: "#ff00aa"`**. Hot magenta matches nothing in the palette and reads like a debug colour left in. I've assumed it was scaffolding and used `bg-surface` above. If it's intentional it needs a named token (`accent`) — that's a design call, not a mechanical one, so flag it rather than silently pick.

### 3. `responsive_fragility` (low, conf 0.65) — line 10/24

The detector's `fix_shape` says to drive layout from container queries, not pixel media queries, and that's right for a `Card`: it's a component reused at unknown widths, so it should respond to *its own* box, not the viewport. That's why the code above uses `@container` + `@2xl:` rather than adding an `@media` block.

Concretely: `w-full max-w-4xl` replaces `width: 800`/`maxWidth: 1024` (and resolves the contradiction — one constraint, not two); the fixed `720`/`240` columns become a single-column grid that promotes to `minmax(0,1fr) 15rem` only when the card is wide enough, with `min-w-0` so long content can't blow the track out; `fontSize: 18/14` become `text-lg`/`text-sm` on the rem scale. This also kills the 960-in-800 overflow, since the columns are now fractional.

## Sequencing and verification

Do **2 before 1 and 3** — extend the tokens first, so the markup rewrite has something to reference and you never have a commit that hard-codes values in `className`.

Because `test_gap` is 1.0 with zero test files, nothing will catch a regression here. The a11y change is the one that needs a test, since it's the only finding with user-visible consequences: render with `onSelect`, assert the control is reachable by role and fires on keyboard activation. Then re-run `crimes context src/Card.tsx` — all three should clear. Note that if you keep inline `style` and merely swap in token *variables*, `design_token_escape` may still fire on whatever raw values remain in JSX; the class-based version is what makes it go quiet.

One caveat on the plan as specified: I haven't applied any of this. `evals/fixtures/07-stress-frontend` is scanner input — editing it would move the eval baseline and require a patch bump plus an `evals/results/` re-run per the repo's versioning policy. Say the word if you want these edits applied to the fixture (or to a real `Card.tsx` elsewhere) and I'll make them.
